    "narrative": "The Austin Waves enter today\u2019s slate with a clear emphasis on exploiting favorable matchups against secondary pitching, anchored by **Kyle Tucker** (75.29) and **Andy Pages** (70.97). **Kyle Tucker** stands as our premier play; he avoids the high-end aces facing the rest of the league and receives a meaningful lift from a platoon advantage against Brandon Pfaadt. Meanwhile, **Andy Pages** is set up for a productive outing, buoyed by the prospect of Pfaadt\u2019s slow delivery, which significantly improves his efficiency in stealing bags\u2014a crucial metric for our 5x5 standing. **Jacob Wilson** (69.79) also earns a high-confidence start, capitalizing on a favorable park factor and a strong order placement that keeps him well above the 60-point threshold despite the inherent volatility of daily projections.\n\nWhile the core is set, we are exercising extreme caution regarding the \"Lineup Pending\" status for several key contributors. **Gunnar Henderson** (60.75) presents the most significant situational risk today; while his talent is undeniable, he is drawing a nightmare matchup against Jacob deGrom. His score is propped up largely by his leadoff spot and platoon splits, but any regression in his efficiency against elite velocity could render this start a liability. On the bench, **Sam Antonacci** (46.73) was the closest candidate to cracking the lineup. Ultimately, the optimizer prioritized the higher ceiling of **Wade Meckler** (49.99), who benefits from a cleaner path to runs and stolen bases at the top of the order compared to the situational squeeze facing **Sam Antonacci** against a formidable Gavin Williams. \n\nThe strategy for today remains focused on balancing elite talent with the specific goal of maximizing stolen base opportunities. **Luis Robert Jr.** (63.18) and **Jackson Merrill** (66.07) are both elevated by the potential for high-volume base stealing against Bubba Chandler and Peter Lambert, respectively, whose slow deliveries are projected to play directly into our hands. Conversely, **Heliot Ramos** (48.93) is walking a thin line; although he is a serviceable option, facing Chris Sale is an uphill battle that limits his upside. We will be monitoring the final lineup confirmations closely, specifically for **Liam Hicks** and **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.**, to ensure the projected batting order holds before the first pitch.",
